 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Authorizes the commissioner of health to make grants to not-for-profit organizations and elementary, secondary and postsecondary schools to be used to help pay for the costs of conducting local blood drives.",
 "title": "Authorizes the commissioner of health to make grants to be used to help pay for the costs of conducting local blood drives",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A2381",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A2381-2017",
     "printNo": "A2381",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 273\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 2381, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the public health law, in relation to authorizing\n      the commissioner of health to make grants to be used for the costs\n      of conducting a local blood donation drive\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would authorize the funding of grants to  non-profit  organ-\nizations and schools to help pay for the costs of conducting local blood\ndonation  drives. However, there is no funding associated with this bill\nto support any grant; and it duplicates work already being performed  by\nthe Department of Health (DOH).\n \n  DOH  currently supports appeals for increased donation during seasonal\nor weather-related shortages, and also  works  with  other  agencies  to\ndistribute  mailings  that  encourage blood donation and provide contact\ninformation for blood centers. DOH also collects data on behalf of blood\nbanks on the number of units of  blood  collected  and  transfused,  and\nprovides  administrative  and  technical  support  to the New York State\nCouncil on Human Blood and Transfusion Services, which is  charged  with\nmaximizing blood safety in New York.\n \n  While  I  fully  support  and encourage blood donation and local blood\ndrives, this bill would result in increased and unbudgeted costs to  the\nState that must be addressed in the context of the overall State budget.\nFurthermore,  DOH  actively  supports  and  promotes blood donation, and\nmaintains and enhances blood supply safety, including providing informa-\ntion to the public in support of blood donation and collecting  data  on\nblood collection and transfusion. Accordingly, while I am constrained to\ndisapprove  this bill, I will direct DOH to continue its ongoing efforts\nto encourage and support local blood donation across the State.  I  also\nencourage  all  New  Yorkers  who  are  eligible to donate to do so, and\nfurther acknowledge and commend the efforts of volunteers throughout the\nState who work with organizations that sponsor local blood drives.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 273,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2018
   }
  ],
